WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.850
The source editor offers navigation, completion and
diagnostic tools that help maintain a diagram as it grows.

00:00:07.970 --> 00:00:13.195
Use them to find an element, understand an error and
review a proposed repair.

00:00:13.315 --> 00:00:17.365
An offered completion or quick fix still needs your
judgment.

00:00:17.485 --> 00:00:26.660
In this lesson we locate commands, search the source,
inspect an intentional error, and connect editor
assistance to the semantic review workflow.

00:00:30.167 --> 00:00:34.617
Open Commands and type a few words describing the action
you need.

00:00:34.737 --> 00:00:39.587
The palette filters available commands and shows their
current context.

00:00:39.707 --> 00:00:43.582
Use the arrow keys to choose an entry and Enter to run it.

00:00:43.702 --> 00:00:51.027
Read disabled commands in context: the diagram type,
selection or permission may explain why they are
unavailable.

00:00:51.147 --> 00:00:55.147
Escape closes the palette when you are only looking up a
command.

00:00:58.667 --> 00:01:04.242
Use the diagram outline when you want to navigate by
element rather than by line number.

00:01:04.362 --> 00:01:09.387
Search for a meaningful label and use the type filter to
narrow a large model.

00:01:09.507 --> 00:01:14.032
Selecting a result connects navigation to the diagram and
source.

00:01:14.152 --> 00:01:19.152
Keep aliases and labels clear so search results remain
understandable.

00:01:19.272 --> 00:01:28.597
For exact text, use the source editor search instead; a
text match and a semantic element are related but
different ways of locating information.

00:01:32.100 --> 00:01:37.375
A source-first workflow also supports constructs outside
the visual editors.

00:01:37.495 --> 00:01:43.645
This state diagram renders successfully, while Issues
identifies four preserved source lines.

00:01:43.765 --> 00:01:47.465
Open Preserved source to inspect the explanation.

00:01:47.585 --> 00:01:55.410
Preserved means the source remains intact; it does not
promise that the current visual editor understands every
statement.

00:01:55.530 --> 00:02:04.305
Edit this example in the source, verify the current
preview, and use a supported diagram type when you need
its dedicated visual controls.

00:02:07.833 --> 00:02:11.258
Open the diagram outline and search for Design.

00:02:11.378 --> 00:02:15.678
Dock the outline so the match list remains alongside the
editor.

00:02:15.798 --> 00:02:22.123
Select the Design task result to move to its source
statement and reveal the corresponding object.

00:02:22.243 --> 00:02:27.368
Check the task identity before editing when several
results share a label.

00:02:27.488 --> 00:02:33.338
Docking and searching change your workspace navigation;
they do not change the authored schedule.

00:02:33.458 --> 00:02:37.558
Use the close control when you need the full drawing area
again.

00:02:41.067 --> 00:02:45.292
Click in the editor and use the search shortcut to find
Design.

00:02:45.412 --> 00:02:50.537
Source search is useful for exact labels, aliases and
repeated syntax.

00:02:50.657 --> 00:02:57.932
Check every replacement before applying it broadly,
especially when a word also appears in notes or display
text.

00:02:58.052 --> 00:03:04.527
Semantic rename is the better route when the intent is to
rename a recognized referenced element.

00:03:04.647 --> 00:03:09.947
After a change, inspect both the declarations and the
references that should follow it.

00:03:13.467 --> 00:03:18.117
Open Format source and read the preview before applying a
text change.

00:03:18.237 --> 00:03:25.962
This aliased task example is not supported by the
formatter, so the dialog explicitly reports that nothing
was changed.

00:03:26.082 --> 00:03:32.982
For a supported simple task example, inspect how the
proposed formatting removes leading statement whitespace.

00:03:33.102 --> 00:03:39.677
Apply the reviewed result, then verify that the task
statements remain and the diagram preview is current.

00:03:39.797 --> 00:03:46.297
Use shared Undo and confirm that the original indentation
returns as one undoable change.

00:03:46.417 --> 00:03:52.442
Leave unsupported syntax unchanged and keep formatting
separate from semantic edits.

00:03:55.967 --> 00:04:02.392
We will deliberately replace a task duration with an
invalid word in this disposable example.

00:04:02.512 --> 00:04:09.587
Wait for the diagnostics, then use Next error and Explain
error to inspect the reported location and guidance.

00:04:09.707 --> 00:04:17.582
Keep the last good preview in mind while a new render is
invalid; a visible picture does not mean the current
source has succeeded.

00:04:17.702 --> 00:04:24.777
Use Go to line to visit the reported statement, then
repair the authored statement and wait for Preview current
again.

00:04:28.300 --> 00:04:31.700
The repair is to restore a numeric duration.

00:04:31.820 --> 00:04:37.695
Return to the source and replace the invalid word with
five, then wait for Preview current.

00:04:37.815 --> 00:04:42.565
Check that the diagnostic clears and that the expected
task is visible.

00:04:42.685 --> 00:04:49.735
When a quick fix is offered, inspect its proposed source
change and the resulting diagnostics before applying it.

00:04:49.855 --> 00:04:58.055
Safe batch fixes can help with independent changes, but do
not combine ambiguous alternatives simply to make the
warnings disappear.

00:05:01.567 --> 00:05:06.992
Completion helps insert supported syntax and known
identifiers at the cursor.

00:05:07.112 --> 00:05:14.062
Review the selected suggestion before accepting it,
because the right text still depends on the surrounding
statement.

00:05:14.182 --> 00:05:20.207
Semantic highlighting and references help you follow a
recognized identity through the source.

00:05:20.327 --> 00:05:26.502
Use rename for a recognized symbol, review its proposed
scope, and verify the rendered result.

00:05:26.622 --> 00:05:35.372
If a construct is not understood semantically, inspect and
edit its text deliberately instead of assuming every
mention will be updated.

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:43.225
Place the cursor inside the design alias and invoke
Rename.

00:05:43.345 --> 00:05:49.520
The preview distinguishes the declaration from references
and leaves comments and notes unchanged.

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:56.815
Enter interaction underscore design as a compact alias
without spaces, then inspect the proposed edits.

00:05:56.935 --> 00:06:01.910
Apply the rename and verify the declaration and downstream
dependency.

00:06:02.030 --> 00:06:10.055
A semantic rename changes the recognized identity
consistently; it is different from replacing every
matching word in the file.

00:06:13.567 --> 00:06:18.792
Completion proposes existing tasks and supported
statements at the cursor.

00:06:18.912 --> 00:06:25.137
In this disposable example, begin a bracketed reference
with Des and open the completion list.

00:06:25.257 --> 00:06:32.432
Inspect the Design suggestion before accepting it, then
complete the statement to set progress to twenty five
percent.

00:06:32.552 --> 00:06:38.277
Check that the accepted reference uses the task alias and
that the current preview agrees.

00:06:38.397 --> 00:06:45.397
Use completion to reduce typing, then read the result
rather than assuming a suggestion understands the whole
model.

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:55.300
Remove the closing delimiter in a disposable copy and
inspect the resulting diagnostic.

00:06:55.420 --> 00:07:00.120
Open the available source fixes and read the proposed
replacement.

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:07.290
Apply the closing delimiter repair, then wait for the
current preview and verify that the task source remains
intact.

00:07:07.410 --> 00:07:13.585
A safe repair addresses this recognized issue; it does not
certify that the plan is correct.

00:07:13.705 --> 00:07:20.005
Use Undo if the result does not match your intention, and
keep ambiguous alternatives separate.

00:07:23.533 --> 00:07:27.658
Sequence diagrams also support semantic change review.

00:07:27.778 --> 00:07:34.203
A participant edit and its related messages may form
changes that should be accepted together.

00:07:34.323 --> 00:07:40.298
Use Version history, select the baseline, and inspect
Review alongside Source and Rendered.

00:07:40.418 --> 00:07:47.518
Select confirmed related groups, validate the combined
result and review replacement warnings before applying.

00:07:47.638 --> 00:07:51.513
If a change remains unclassified, use Source review.

00:07:51.633 --> 00:07:57.258
The history lesson demonstrates the same workflow and how
to export review evidence.

00:08:00.767 --> 00:08:06.817
Keep the current Sequence diagram as the review base and
import a proposed comparison source.

00:08:06.937 --> 00:08:13.262
The proposal renames the Web app display label and changes
its final confirmation message.

00:08:13.382 --> 00:08:19.182
Inspect both recognized groups, select them together and
review their combined validity.

00:08:19.302 --> 00:08:26.502
Apply the two selected groups, then verify that both the
participant label and message changed in the working
source.

00:08:26.622 --> 00:08:30.922
The original alias and message endpoints remain
consistent.

00:08:31.042 --> 00:08:36.917
This is grouped acceptance of a proposed edit, not a blind
replacement of the whole file.

00:08:40.433 --> 00:08:46.683
Pause and locate a task using the outline, then find its
exact statement using source search.

00:08:46.803 --> 00:08:53.353
Introduce one invalid duration in the practice copy,
navigate to the error, explain it and repair it.

00:08:53.473 --> 00:08:59.148
Try completion in a supported statement and inspect the
suggestion before acceptance.

00:08:59.268 --> 00:09:04.418
Rename a referenced element with the semantic workflow and
review the result.

00:09:04.538 --> 00:09:10.838
Success means you can explain why the source is valid and
why the model still expresses your intent.

00:09:14.367 --> 00:09:19.817
Use editor assistance to reduce searching and make changes
easier to inspect.

00:09:19.937 --> 00:09:26.887
Read diagnostics rather than guessing from the picture,
review proposed repairs and verify the current render.

00:09:27.007 --> 00:09:31.457
Keep semantic tools and exact source review available
together.

00:09:31.577 --> 00:09:38.902
Next we will preserve known states in history and use
comparisons to make changes understandable to another
reviewer.
